package theme

import (
	"image/color"
	"testing"
)

// DEVDESK-751, AC7 (decision Q1-A): the Fix block of a finding detail is written
// in ColorText alone, its labels in DetailLabelStyle (bold, ColorText), and the
// word "Warning:" carries the warning. ColorDim, ColorSubTitleFg, ColorTitleFg,
// ColorWarn and ColorFooterError fall under 4.5:1 on several of the 13 shipped
// themes (measured on 2026-11-23), so none of them is used there. ColorDim is
// left as it is: it marks a greyed key, an inactive component that WCAG 1.4.3
// exempts.

// detailMinContrast is WCAG 1.4.3 for text.
const detailMinContrast = 4.5

// Every style of the block reads on every shipped theme.
func TestTheFixBlockStylesReachTextContrastOnEveryTheme(t *testing.T) {
	eachShippedTheme(t, func(t *testing.T, name string) {
		for _, ink := range []struct {
			what string
			fg   color.Color
		}{
			{"ColorText", ColorText},
			{"DetailLabelStyle", DetailLabelStyle.GetForeground()},
		} {
			ratio, err := ContrastRatio(ink.fg, ColorBackground)
			if err != nil {
				t.Errorf("theme %s: %s: %v", name, ink.what, err)
				continue
			}
			if ratio < detailMinContrast {
				t.Errorf("theme %s: %s %s on the background %s is %.2f:1, want >= %.1f:1",
					name, ink.what, Hex(ink.fg), Hex(ColorBackground), ratio, detailMinContrast)
			}
		}
	})
}

// The label is bold, in the text colour, on the theme's background, and it follows
// the theme that is applied: a style declared once, at the top of styles.go, keeps
// the colours of the default theme after ApplyTheme.
func REDACTED(t *testing.T) {
	eachShippedTheme(t, func(t *testing.T, name string) {
		if !DetailLabelStyle.GetBold() {
			t.Errorf("theme %s: DetailLabelStyle is not bold: the label would carry nothing but its colour", name)
		}
		if got := DetailLabelStyle.GetForeground(); !sameColor(got, ColorText) {
			t.Errorf("theme %s: DetailLabelStyle foreground = %s, want ColorText %s", name, Hex(got), Hex(ColorText))
		}
		if got := DetailLabelStyle.GetBackground(); !sameColor(got, ColorBackground) {
			t.Errorf("theme %s: DetailLabelStyle background = %s, want ColorBackground %s", name, Hex(got), Hex(ColorBackground))
		}
	})
}
		out[strings.TrimSuffix(filepath.Base(f), ".json")] = &th
	}
	return out
}

// eachShippedTheme applies every shipped theme in turn, starting from the
// default as the app does, and restores the default afterwards.
// Global colour state: callers must not use t.Parallel.
func eachShippedTheme(t *testing.T, fn func(t *testing.T, name string)) {
	t.Helper()
	themes := shippedThemes(t)
	names := make([]string, 0, len(themes))
	for n := range themes {
		names = append(names, n)
	}
	sort.Strings(names)
	t.Cleanup(func() { ApplyTheme(DefaultTheme()) })
	for _, n := range names {
		t.Run(n, func(t *testing.T) {
			ApplyTheme(DefaultTheme())
			ApplyTheme(themes[n])
			fn(t, n)
		})
	}
}

// ColorPaletteBorder is a closed alias of ColorPrimary: no theme key exists
// for it, so a user theme cannot break the 3:1 border contrast unseen.
func TestPaletteBorderIsAClosedAliasOfPrimary(t *testing.T) {
	eachShippedTheme(t, func(t *testing.T, name string) {
		if ColorPaletteBorder != ColorPrimary {
			t.Errorf("theme %s: ColorPaletteBorder = %q, want ColorPrimary %q", name, ColorPaletteBorder, ColorPrimary)
		}
	})
}

# What DevDesk's own child processes look like: native scan, container scan, clone (DEVDESK-712, S-03 spike)

Measurement note of 2026-11-23. Spike S-03 of the `safe-process-stop` epic (`docs/product/safe-process-stop/`).
It produces no production code and no test: only this note. The harnesses that took the numbers are throwaway
programs kept outside the repository (a separate Go module in the session scratchpad); their behavior is
described below so the numbers can be redone. A dated measure is never corrected afterwards: if a later change
moves these numbers, add a new note.

Every statement below carries one of three labels:

- **Measured**: a number or an observation from a run of the harness on this machine.
- **Read in code**: from reading the repository (path and line at commit `ec457184`) or the gopsutil v4.26.7 source.
- **Unverified**: recalled, deduced or expected, and not checked here. Never to be quoted as a fact.

| | |
|---|---|
| Question | what does a "processes linked to DevDesk" screen see of what DevDesk starts, and is it worth building (H6, H7 of the brief)? |
| Machine | Linux 6.12.107 (Debian 13), 22 logical cores, 657 processes, load average about 0.7 at the start, go1.27.4, git 2.47.3, gitleaks 8.30.1, trivy 0.76.2, gopsutil v4.26.7 (the version of `go.mod`). Not a dedicated machine |
| Harness | a Go program that plays DevDesk: it starts a command through `exec.CommandContext` with `Start` then `Wait` (the shape of `cliRunner.Run`), and while the command runs it polls its own process with gopsutil `Process.Children()`, recursively, recording pid, ppid, name, command line, first and last sighting |
| Docker | the `docker` CLI is installed, but the user of this session cannot reach the daemon (`permission denied ... /var/run/docker.sock`, not in the `docker` group). Nothing was run against it and the permission was not worked around. **Scenario (b) is therefore not measured.** Podman is not installed |
| Windows, macOS | no machine available: **not measured** (see their section) |
| Not touched | `~/.devdesk`, any existing container or image, any process that the harness did not start itself |

## What `Children()` is, by reading the code

Read in code, gopsutil v4.26.7:

- **Linux** (`process_linux.go:341`): `Children()` globs `/proc/[0-9]*/stat` and reads every file, keeps those whose
  ppid equals the pid, then builds a `Process` for each. It does not read `/proc/<pid>/task/*/children`. One call
  therefore costs one read per process on the machine.

